\details{
This package exists to answer the question "how does an R graphics
device actually work?" by building one from scratch against R's public
graphics device API (see \code{R_ext/GraphicsDevice.h} and
\code{R_ext/GraphicsEngine.h} in the R source tree, or
\code{vignette("grDevices")}-adjacent material). It is not meant to compete
with mature canvas-based devices such as the archived \code{canvas} package
or \code{webr::canvas()} -- it deliberately keeps things simple (no raster
image support, approximate text metrics, a single rectangular clip
region at a time) so the source stays easy to read end to end.

The package's C source, in \verb{src/}, is organised by responsibility:
\itemize{
\item \code{device.c} -- the device lifecycle: creating it, closing it, starting
new pages. This is the best starting point for understanding how a
device registers itself with R.
\item \code{draw.c} -- the drawing callbacks: lines, rectangles, circles,
polygons, text, clipping.
\item \code{colour.c} -- translating R's packed colour integers, line styles,
and font descriptions into CSS/JS.
\item \code{write.c} -- the HTML/JS text templates everything else writes into.
}

See \code{\link[=cnvs]{cnvs()}} to open a device.
